<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">What it hears</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Bravo listens continuously on 1090&nbsp;MHz and decodes ADS-B
    position messages in Extended Squitter (DF17) format.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>It receives.</h3>
      <p>Airliners, business jets, helicopters and touring aircraft with an ADS-B out transponder.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>It does not receive.</h3>
      <p>It does not receive signals from transponders without ADS-B out, or UAT, TIS-B or ADS-R signals. It does not receive ADS-L or FLARM either. A Go receives those.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Bravo with your phone</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>In your app.</h3>
      <p>It sends traffic over Bluetooth as FLARM NMEA, the same format as a Go, to one navigation app: SkyDemon, SDVFR or another app that supports that format.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>An alarm you feel.</h3>
      <p>Its buzzer and vibration motor warn you of approaching ADS-B traffic.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Not a transmitter.</h3>
      <p>It does not broadcast your position. To be seen in ADS-L, add a Go.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Bravo with a Go</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Paired with a Go, Bravo passes ADS-B traffic to it. The Go
    shows ADS-B, ADS-L and FLARM on one radar. The Go keeps broadcasting your
    position in ADS-L. Together they make a skyBlip Tango.
  </p>
  <p class="more"><%= link_to_page "skyblip-tango", label: "What a Tango does" %></p>
</section>

<section id="status" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Where it stands</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>The hardware.</h3>
      <p>On sale at Seeed Studio. It ships with Meshtastic, a messaging firmware that receives no ADS-B.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>The firmware.</h3>
      <p>Open source. The firmware runs on the X1. Install it by USB-C using the steps below.</p>
      <p class="note"><a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/issues/184">Follow it on GitHub</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Updates.</h3>
      <p>Later updates come from Briefing over Bluetooth, as for the Go.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="install" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">A Bravo, ready in three steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps-grid steps-grid--sequence">
    <li>
      <h3>Connect.</h3>
      <p>Hold the button, plug in the USB-C cable and keep holding until the LED turns white, about 3 seconds. A drive named <span class="mono">X1</span> appears.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Save Meshtastic.</h3>
      <p>Copy <span class="mono">CURRENT.UF2</span> from the <span class="mono">X1</span> drive to your computer first. Dragging that file back onto the drive restores Meshtastic.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Install.</h3>
      <p>Copy the .uf2 file of the latest skyBlip Bravo release from the <a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/releases">releases page</a> to the <span class="mono">X1</span> drive. Bravo needs no activation. In Briefing, set your aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address to leave your own transponder out of the traffic.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

